  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Transforming Shifted Unit Steps",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert each shifted unit step $u(t-a)$ into the Laplace-domain factor $e^{-as}/s$, interpreting the exponential factor as the delay marker used in piecewise-signal transforms.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Transforming Shifted Unit Steps",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Transforming Shifted Unit Steps",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In one-sided Laplace work, the anchor object is the causal step $u(t)$, whose transform is read as $L\\{u(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s}$. That base pair is the template your future shifted-step rules must reuse.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "L\\{u(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A shifted unit step is written as $u(t-a)$ with $a\\ge 0$. The value of $a$ is the time offset where the signal turns on; this same $a$ is the multiplier in the exponential delay factor in the Laplace result.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The direct rule is: if a signal matches $u(t-a)$, then $u(t-a) \\leftrightarrow \\frac{e^{-as}}{s}$. This is the exact move for this lesson: read the shift in time notation and immediately write the exponential delay marker in $s$-space.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "L\\{u(t-a)\\}=\\frac{e^{-as}}{s},\\quad a\\ge 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the base case pattern to stay consistent: for $a=0$, $u(t)$ gives $\\frac{1}{s}$, and for $a=2$, $u(t-2)$ gives $\\frac{e^{-2s}}{s}$. The structure is unchanged; only the delay $a$ in $u(t-a)$ changes the exponential term.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Transforming Shifted Unit Steps",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Transforming a Single Shifted Unit Step",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the one-sided Laplace transform of $u(t-2)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue in this example is the shifted-step form $u(t-a)$, so the delay parameter is read directly from the argument and the signal is recognized as a one-sided step shifted by $a=2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the delayed-step transform pair for one-sided Laplace transforms: $u(t-a) \\leftrightarrow \\frac{e^{-as}}{s}$, with $a\\ge 0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t-a)\\}=\\frac{e^{-as}}{s},\\quad a\\ge 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the pair with $a=2$ by substitution into the rule.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t-2)\\}=\\frac{e^{-2s}}{s}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Final check: the exponent is negative ($e^{-2s}$), and the denominator remains $s$ because the base transform is the unit step, consistent with one-sided Laplace form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Transforming Shifted Unit Steps",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Matching the Shift Amount to the Exponential Factor",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the inverse Laplace transform of $e^{-3s}/s$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This example switches direction from the prior forward-transform work: we now start with a Laplace-domain expression and identify the delayed-step time signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the factorized form $e^{-as}/s$, so we match it to the pair $\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\{e^{-as}/s\\}=u(t-a)$ with $a \\ge 0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\{e^{-as}/s\\}=u(t-a),\\quad a\\ge 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read the exponent as the delay marker: $e^{-3s}$ means $a=3$. The remaining factor is the base transform $1/s$, the one-sided transform of $u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\{e^{-3s}/s\\}=u(t-3)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Final result: $\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\{e^{-3s}/s\\}=u(t-3)$. This keeps a negative sign in the exponent and maps to a delay to the right by 3 seconds, as the one-sided convention requires.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Transforming Shifted Unit Steps",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Transforming Scaled Shifted Unit Steps",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the one-sided Laplace transform of $-2u(t-4)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This step keeps the same delay pattern and adds one new wrinkle: a scalar multiplier outside the shifted step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the solution template from previous steps to identify the delayed step template and the delay index. Here the signal is $-2u(t-4)=(-2)\\cdot u(t-4)$, so the shifted-step part is $u(t-4)$ with $a=4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the delayed-step pair to the step component first: $u(t-a) \\leftrightarrow e^{-as}/s$ with $a\\ge 0$, so $u(t-4) \\leftrightarrow e^{-4s}/s$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "u(t-a) \\leftrightarrow \\frac{e^{-as}}{s},\\quad a\\ge 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Then keep the outside constant as a multiplicative gain and attach it to the transform result: $\\mathcal{L}\\{-2u(t-4)\\} = -2\\,\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t-4)\\}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{-2u(t-4)\\} = -2\\cdot\\frac{e^{-4s}}{s}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Final result: $\\mathcal{L}\\{-2u(t-4)\\}=-\\dfrac{2e^{-4s}}{s}$. The delay marker $e^{-4s}$ and the coefficient $-2$ both remain, so neither the direction of the delay nor the one-sided step support is changed.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Transforming Shifted Unit Steps",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Transforming Sums of Shifted Unit Steps",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the one-sided Laplace transform of $u(t-1)+2u(t-3)-u(t-5)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This example adds one new wrinkle: the signal is a sum of shifted steps, but each term still uses the same delayed-step pattern.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Split the signal into separate terms and identify each delay: $u(t-1)$ with $a=1$, $2u(t-3)$ with $a=3$, and $-u(t-5)$ with $a=5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For each term, apply the delayed-step pair $u(t-a) \\leftrightarrow e^{-as}/s$, which is valid for one-sided transforms.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "u(t-1) \\leftrightarrow \\frac{e^{-s}}{s},\\quad 2u(t-3) \\leftrightarrow 2\\frac{e^{-3s}}{s},\\quad -u(t-5) \\leftrightarrow -\\frac{e^{-5s}}{s}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now combine by linearity, keeping each delay and denominator separate so each term stays in the same transformed form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t-1)+2u(t-3)-u(t-5)\\}=\\frac{e^{-s}}{s}+\\frac{2e^{-3s}}{s}-\\frac{e^{-5s}}{s}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Final result: $\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t-1)+2u(t-3)-u(t-5)\\}=\\dfrac{e^{-s}+2e^{-3s}-e^{-5s}}{s}$. Each term preserves its own delay marker and coefficient, with only the algebraic summation happening in the transform domain.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
